                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Placement is a very simple puzzle game, but I think the idea behind it is much cleverer than the presentation makes it look. The whole game is based around chess pieces, except I am not actually playing chess. Instead, I have to place pieces on a board so that every piece is protected exactly once. That one rule is basically the entire foundation of the game, but it gives me a surprisingly good reason to stop and think about every move before I make it.\n\nWhat I like most is how quickly I understand the basic idea. I do not need to know how to play a full game of chess to get started, although knowing how the pieces move definitely helps. Once I place a piece, I have to think about which other pieces it is protecting and whether I have accidentally protected something more than once. A move that looks correct at first can completely ruin the arrangement somewhere else, so the puzzles gradually make me pay much closer attention to the board.\n\nThere are more than 80 levels, and I like the way the difficulty builds rather than throwing the hardest puzzles at me straight away. The early levels are simple enough for me to understand the rules, while later ones require much more planning. I can take my time too, since there are no time limits, which suits this type of puzzle perfectly. I would rather sit and work something out properly than be forced to rush because of a timer. The relaxing music also fits the quiet nature of the game.\n\nThe biggest weakness is that the game is extremely focused. There is basically one puzzle concept being explored over the entire experience, so once I understand the rules there is not much else to distract me. That is not necessarily a bad thing, but it means the game depends almost entirely on how much I enjoy this particular type of logic puzzle. I also found that some later levels can become quite frustrating because one misplaced piece can force me to rethink almost the entire board.\n\nThe presentation is very minimal, but I think that works reasonably well here. I do not need detailed graphics when the board and chess pieces are all I really need to understand the puzzle. The lack of unnecessary effects also keeps my attention on the actual problem in front of me. I like that the game does not try to make a simple puzzle concept feel bigger than it is. It knows exactly what it wants to be and sticks to it.\n\nThere are also Steam achievements that give me a few extra goals to work towards, including achievements connected to different pieces and completing puzzles in particular ways. I would not say they add a huge amount of depth, but they give me another reason to pay attention to how efficiently I solve certain levels. The fact that the game is completely free also makes the limited presentation and narrow focus much easier for me to accept.\n\nOverall, Placement is a small but well thought out puzzle game that does a lot with a very simple rule. I like how easy it is to understand, how the puzzles gradually become more demanding and how there is no pressure to rush through them. The lack of variety is the main thing stopping it from getting one of my higher ratings, because once I have had enough of the central mechanic there is not much else to keep me playing. Still, when I want a quiet logic game that makes me properly think without filling the screen with unnecessary features, Placement does exactly what I want.\n\nRating: 7/10\n",
